--TEST--
A `spawn script` child given `limits:` runs under that sub-cap rather than the tree's: it is stopped where its own ceiling is, its parent goes on running with `ok = false`, and the same allocation the child was stopped for succeeds in the parent — `rule:security/isolate-budget-is-the-trees`, `rule:errors/on-limit` and `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`: a case with no configuration
# cannot spawn at all.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true

# The tree's own ceiling, wide enough that everything below is the sub-cap's
# doing and not this number's — `rule:security/isolate-budget-is-the-trees`.
[limits]
memory = "256M"
--FILE greedy.nvs--
<?nvs
// Well past the `limits:` this child was spawned under and well inside the
// tree's own, so what stops it is the sub-cap alone. A limit breach is
// `rule:errors/on-limit`'s `FATAL`, which no `catch` in here sees — it arrives
// on the parent's side as `ok = false` instead.
array<int> $rows = [];
for (var $i = 0; $i < 400000; $i = $i + 1) {
    $rows[] = $i;
}
echo "unreachable", "\n";
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `output: 'capture'` for the reason a-child-shares-nothing-with-its-parent
// gives: the child's lines arrive here as a value the parent prints, so their
// order against the parent's own is a fact rather than a race.
var $child = spawn script "greedy.nvs" with(limits: {memory: "1M"}, output: "capture");
var $done = await $child;
echo "child ok=", $done->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";

// The parent is still running, and still under the tree's own ceiling rather
// than the child's: the sub-cap was the child's to spend, and a child spending
// it did not narrow anything here.
array<int> $rows = [];
for (var $i = 0; $i < 400000; $i = $i + 1) {
    $rows[] = $i;
}
echo "parent held ", Core\Arr::count($rows), "\n";
--EXPECT--
child ok=false
parent held 400000
